g('fcl-vs-lcl-shipping', 'sea', 'FCL vs LCL|which to choose',
  'FCL or LCL? The difference between full container load and less than container load, costs, transit time, risk and when each makes sense.',
  'FCL means you rent the whole container. LCL means your cargo shares a container with other shippers. The right choice depends on volume.',
  [('FCL', ['You get the entire container and the seal stays on from loading to delivery.', 'Faster, with less handling and a lower risk of damage.', 'Best when you fill more than roughly half a 20 ft container.']),
   ('LCL', ['You pay by volume or weight, whichever is greater, in cubic metres or tonnes.', 'Cargo is consolidated at a CFS at origin and deconsolidated at the destination CFS.', 'Slower and with more handling, but cheaper for small lots.']),
   ('Rule of thumb', 'Compare the LCL cost for your cubic metres with the FCL cost. Often a 20 ft box is better value from about 12 to 15 cubic metres up. Our CBM calculator and load planner help you check.'),
   ('Other points', 'LCL has extra destination CFS charges and strict cut-off times. FCL lets you load at your own factory.')],
  [('Is LCL riskier?', 'There is more handling, so a higher risk of damage and delay. Good packing helps.'),
   ('Can I mix cargo from many suppliers in LCL?', 'Yes. That is called buyers consolidation and it can cut costs.'),
   ('What is a CBM?', 'A cubic metre. LCL is billed by CBM or by tonne, whichever pays more.')],
  ('cbm-calculator', 'Open the CBM calculator'))
g('how-lcl-consolidation-works', 'sea', 'How LCL consolidation works|from CFS to CFS',
  'How LCL shipping works: booking, CFS stuffing, master and house BL, deconsolidation and the charges at each end.',
  'In LCL, a consolidator fills a container with cargo from many shippers and breaks it up at the destination.',
  [('Origin', ['You book with a consolidator or forwarder and deliver the cargo to the CFS before the cut-off.', 'The CFS receives, measures and loads it into the container. The consolidator issues a house BL to you.']),
   ('At sea', 'The consolidated container sails under one master BL in the name of the consolidator.'),
   ('Destination', ['The container goes to the destination CFS.', 'The cargo is unloaded and sorted. You clear customs and take delivery against the house BL or the delivery order.']),
   ('Costs to expect', 'Freight per cubic metre, origin and destination CFS handling, documentation and the delivery order fee. Minimum charges apply for small lots.')],
  [('How long does LCL take?', 'Usually a week or more longer than FCL, because of stuffing, deconsolidation and cut-offs.'),
   ('Can LCL carry dangerous goods?', 'Only with approval and special handling, and at higher cost.'),
   ('Who is liable for damage?', 'The consolidator, within the limits of the BL terms. Insure the cargo.')],
  ('buyers-consolidation', 'See buyers consolidation'))
g('container-types-explained', 'sea', 'Container types|20 ft, 40 ft, high cube, reefer, open top and flat rack',
  'The main shipping container types: 20 ft and 40 ft, high cube, reefer, open top, flat rack and tank, with sizes and uses.',
  'Most cargo goes in a standard box, but some cargo needs a special one. Here are the common types.',
  [('Standard dry containers', [('table', ['Type', 'Inside length', 'Typical use'], [['20 ft GP', 'about 5.9 m', 'Heavy cargo and small volumes'], ['40 ft GP', 'about 12.0 m', 'General cargo'], ['40 ft HC', 'about 12.0 m, taller', 'Light, bulky cargo']], 'Dimensions vary a little by builder. See our container sizes page for details.')]),
   ('Special types', ['Reefer: refrigerated for food, pharma and chemicals.', 'Open top: no roof, for tall cargo loaded from above.', 'Flat rack: no sides or roof, for heavy and out-of-gauge cargo.', 'Tank: for liquids in bulk.', 'Ventilated and insulated boxes for coffee, cocoa and similar.']),
   ('Choosing', 'Start with the cargo volume and weight. Then check the door size, the payload and the road weight limits. Use our load planner to test your packing list.'),
   ('Payload', 'The payload is the weight the container can carry. For a 20 ft box it is often 25 to 28 tonnes, and road and rail limits in India can be lower.')],
  [('What is a high cube?', 'A container that is about 30 cm taller than a standard one.'),
   ('Can I load a 40 ft HC on a standard trailer?', 'Usually yes, but check the height limit for the route.'),
   ('Where are the exact sizes?', 'On our container sizes page and in the load planner.')],
  ('container-sizes', 'See the container sizes'))
g('reefer-container-guide', 'sea', 'Reefer containers|temperature, pre-cooling and plug-in',
  'How reefer containers work: set point, pre-trip inspection, pre-cooling, loading below the red line, ventilation and plug-in at port.',
  'A reefer is a container with its own cooling unit. It keeps cargo at a set temperature if you do the loading right.',
  [('Before loading', ['Choose the temperature set point and the ventilation setting for the cargo.', 'Ask for a pre-trip inspection (PTI) of the reefer and run it at the right temperature.', 'Pre-cool the cargo. A reefer holds a temperature, it does not chill warm cargo.']),
   ('Loading', ['Load only below the red load line on the container wall.', 'Keep the air flow free: do not block the floor T-bars or the air return.', 'Use proper packaging with vents or stackable cartons.']),
   ('During the trip', 'The container needs power at the terminal, on the truck (a genset) and on the ship. A gap in power breaks the cold chain. Ask for the temperature logger data at the end.'),
   ('Documents', 'Phytosanitary or health certificates, plus the temperature instructions on the BL as the buyer or the bank needs.')],
  [('Can I put frozen and chilled cargo together?', 'Not unless they need the same temperature.'),
   ('Who pays for power at the port?', 'It is included in terminal charges, as per the line and terminal tariff.'),
   ('How much can a reefer carry?', 'Our reefer load calculator shows pallets and cartons for 20 ft and 40 ft HC.')],
  ('reefer-container-load-calculator', 'Open the reefer load calculator'))
g('open-top-flat-rack-out-of-gauge-cargo', 'sea', 'Open top, flat rack and out-of-gauge cargo|how to ship oversize loads',
  'How to ship oversize cargo: open top and flat rack containers, out-of-gauge limits, lashing, surveys and the costs to plan for.',
  'Cargo that is too tall, too wide or too heavy for a standard box needs a special container or break bulk.',
  [('Open top', 'The roof is removable. Cargo is loaded from above by crane. It fits over-height cargo as long as the cargo fits within the width and length.'),
   ('Flat rack', 'No sides or roof. Used for cargo that is too wide, too tall or very heavy. Cargo can overhang the sides or the top, and the line charges for the lost slots.'),
   ('What to plan', ['Exact dimensions and weight of each piece, and the centre of gravity.', 'Lashing and securing, often by a surveyor, to the line rules.', 'Approval from the line before you book, since the ship stowage needs planning.', 'Port and road permits for the move.']),
   ('Costs', 'Out of gauge cargo costs more because it blocks the adjacent slots and needs extra handling and survey. Get a quote early with all the dimensions.')],
  [('What is out of gauge (OOG)?', 'Cargo that goes beyond the standard container dimensions.'),
   ('Do I need a survey?', 'For most OOG and heavy lifts, yes. The line may ask for one.'),
   ('When is break bulk better?', 'When the cargo is very large or very heavy. See our project cargo service.')],
  ('project-cargo', 'See our project cargo service'))
g('ocean-freight-surcharges-explained', 'sea', 'Ocean freight surcharges|BAF, THC, peak season and more',
  'The charges on an ocean freight quote explained: base freight, BAF, THC, documentation, ISPS, peak season and other surcharges.',
  'An ocean quote is more than the base rate. These are the usual lines.',
  [('Origin and freight', [('table', ['Charge', 'What it covers'], [['Ocean freight', 'The base rate for the sea leg'], ['BAF', 'Bunker adjustment, the fuel cost'], ['CAF', 'Currency adjustment, in some trades'], ['THC', 'Terminal handling at the port'], ['Documentation', 'BL and the paperwork fee'], ['ISPS or security', 'Port security surcharge']])]),
   ('Seasonal and trade charges', 'Peak season surcharge, congestion, emergency risk or war risk, low sulphur, and EU emissions trading charges can be added by carriers. These change often.'),
   ('At destination', 'Destination THC, DO fee, CFS charges for LCL, detention and demurrage if the cargo is held too long, and local transport.'),
   ('How to compare', 'Ask for an all-in rate with every charge listed, for the same Incoterm and the same free time. Our freight quote checker helps you spot missing lines.')],
  [('Is the base rate the main cost?', 'Not always. Surcharges can be more than the base rate in some trades.'),
   ('Why do surcharges change?', 'Carriers adjust them for fuel, congestion and demand.'),
   ('Are surcharges negotiable?', 'Some are, particularly for regular volumes.')],
  ('freight-quote-checker', 'Open the freight quote checker'))
g('demurrage-and-detention-explained', 'sea', 'Demurrage and detention|what they are and how to avoid them',
  'The difference between demurrage and detention, how free days work, how the charges build up and how to avoid them.',
  'Both are daily charges for keeping a container too long. They are charged by different parties for different things.',
  [('Demurrage', 'Charged by the port or terminal, or by the shipping line, when the container stays in the port beyond the free days.'),
   ('Detention', 'Charged by the shipping line when you keep its container outside the terminal beyond the free days, before you return it empty.'),
   ('How they add up', 'Each charge is per day and per container, and the rate grows in steps after the first period. A week of delay can cost more than the freight on some routes.'),
   ('How to avoid them', ['Ask for enough free days when you book and get them in writing.', 'Pre-file the bill of entry and get the DO before arrival.', 'Have the transport and the customs broker ready.', 'Return the empty container quickly.', 'Ask for an extension before the free time ends.'])],
  [('Who pays demurrage if customs delays the cargo?', 'Usually the importer, unless the delay is the fault of the carrier or the terminal.'),
   ('Do weekends count?', 'Usually yes, unless the tariff says otherwise.'),
   ('Can charges be waived?', 'Sometimes, with proof of the cause and a request to the line.')],
  ('freight-quote-checker', 'Open the freight quote checker'))
g('vgm-verified-gross-mass-guide', 'sea', 'VGM|Verified Gross Mass explained',
  'What VGM is under SOLAS, who must declare it, the two methods of weighing and the cut-off times for exporters in India.',
  'Since 2016, a packed export container cannot be loaded on a ship without a Verified Gross Mass. The shipper is responsible for it.',
  [('What it is', 'The VGM is the total weight of the container: cargo, packing, dunnage, lashing and the tare weight of the box.'),
   ('Two ways to get it', ['Method 1: weigh the packed container on a calibrated scale.', 'Method 2: weigh all the packages and contents and add the container tare weight, under an approved process.']),
   ('Who declares', 'The shipper named on the BL. The forwarder or terminal can weigh and submit it for you, but the responsibility remains with the shipper.'),
   ('Cut-offs', 'The line sets a VGM cut-off before the vessel sails. Missing it can mean the container is not loaded.')],
  [('Is it needed for LCL?', 'The consolidator declares it for the consolidated container, using the shipper weights.'),
   ('Is there a tolerance?', 'Lines and terminals set a tolerance. Check with yours.'),
   ('Where can I prepare the VGM?', 'Our VGM declaration tool makes the form for you.')],
  ('vgm-declaration', 'Open the VGM tool'))
g('container-cut-off-times-explained', 'sea', 'Container cut-off times|gate-in, SI, VGM and customs',
  'The cut-offs for an export container: gate-in, documents, VGM, shipping bill and how to avoid a rolled container.',
  'Each export container has several deadlines before the vessel sails. Miss one and the box can be rolled to the next ship.',
  [('Typical cut-offs', [('table', ['Cut-off', 'What it is'], [['Gate-in', 'The last time the container can enter the terminal'], ['Documents (SI)', 'Shipping instructions to the carrier'], ['VGM', 'Verified gross mass to the line or terminal'], ['Customs', 'Shipping bill and let export order']])]),
   ('Why timing matters', 'A rolled container waits for the next sailing, costs extra storage and may miss the letter of credit shipment date.'),
   ('Plan back from the sailing', 'Add time for the trip to the port, the customs examination, weighing and any holiday. For ICD cargo, the rail or road to the port takes extra days.'),
   ('Tip', 'Ask your forwarder for the cut-off list at booking time and confirm again two days before.')],
  [('Do cut-offs change?', 'Yes. Lines and terminals can change them if the ship schedule moves.'),
   ('What is a rolled container?', 'A container that is left behind and goes on a later ship.'),
   ('Can I get a late gate-in?', 'Sometimes, for a fee, if the terminal allows.')],
  ('track-shipment', 'Open the tracking hub'))
g('container-seals-and-numbers', 'sea', 'Container numbers and seals|how to read and check them',
  'How to read a container number (ISO 6346), what the check digit is, and what types of seal are used and when to check them.',
  'Every container has a unique number and every export box has a seal. Both go on the documents.',
  [('The container number', 'Four letters (owner code and category), six digits and a check digit, for example ABCU 123456 7. The check digit is calculated from the other ten characters. Our container number checker validates it.'),
   ('Seals', ['Bolt seals: high security, used by lines and customs.', 'Cable seals: for tanks and special loads.', 'Plastic or indicative seals: for lower risk.', 'Customs seals applied by officers after examination.']),
   ('Checks', 'Match the container and seal numbers on the BL, the packing list, the VGM and the gate pass. A mismatch can stop the gate-in or the clearance.'),
   ('Tampering', 'If a seal is broken or different on arrival, note it on the receipt and tell the carrier and the insurer at once.')],
  [('What do the first four letters mean?', 'Three letters for the owner and a fourth for the category, usually U for freight containers.'),
   ('Who puts the seal on?', 'The shipper at stuffing, and customs may add one.'),
   ('Is a seal mandatory?', 'Carriers and customs require one for export containers.')],
  ('container-number-checker', 'Open the container number checker'))
g('what-is-a-freight-forwarder', 'sea', 'What is a freight forwarder|and what do they do',
  'What a freight forwarder does, how it differs from a shipping line, NVOCC and customs broker, and when to use one.',
  'A freight forwarder organises the movement of cargo for you, by sea, air or land. It buys space from carriers and handles the paperwork.',
  [('What a forwarder does', ['Gets quotes and books space on the ship or flight.', 'Arranges pick-up, packing advice and inland transport.', 'Prepares the documents, such as the BL, AWB and certificates.', 'Handles customs clearance directly or through a broker.', 'Tracks the cargo and handles problems.']),
   ('Forwarder, NVOCC and line', [('table', ['Party', 'Role'], [['Shipping line', 'Owns the ships and carries the cargo'], ['NVOCC', 'Carries cargo under its own BL without owning ships'], ['Freight forwarder', 'Arranges the whole move for the shipper'], ['Customs broker', 'Files customs declarations']])]),
   ('Why use one', 'Better rates from volume, knowledge of the paperwork and a single point of contact. For small shippers, it is the normal route.'),
   ('How they are paid', 'By the margin between the carrier rate and what they charge you, plus fees for services. Ask for a clear quote.')],
  [('Is a forwarder the same as a customs broker?', 'No, though one company often does both. Customs brokers must be licensed.'),
   ('Do I need one for a small shipment?', 'Yes, it is usually cheaper and easier.'),
   ('What if the forwarder fails?', 'Check its licence, insurance and references before you book.')],
  ('contact', 'Talk to our team'))
g('how-to-choose-a-freight-forwarder', 'sea', 'How to choose a freight forwarder|10 questions to ask',
  'How to choose a freight forwarder in India: licences, references, quotes, communication, claims handling and the questions to ask before you book.',
  'A cheap quote is not always cheap. Here is how to compare forwarders.',
  [('Check the basics', ['Registered company with a customs broker licence if it clears cargo, and a valid IEC.', 'Membership of trade bodies and, for air, IATA cargo agent status.', 'Years of experience on your trade lane and cargo type.']),
   ('Ask these questions', ['Is the quote all-in, with every charge listed?', 'Which Incoterm and free days does it assume?', 'Who carries the cargo, and is it a direct line booking or a consolidation?', 'Who will handle my account and how do I reach them out of hours?', 'How do you handle a claim, a roll or a delay?', 'Do you provide tracking?']),
   ('Compare like with like', 'Put the quotes side by side: base rate, surcharges, destination costs, transit time and free days. Our freight quote checker helps.'),
   ('Trust your tests', 'Start with a small shipment. Watch how the forwarder handles the paperwork and the questions.')],
  [('Is the cheapest the best?', 'Seldom. Hidden charges and poor service cost more than a small saving.'),
   ('Should I use more than one?', 'Many shippers use two for different lanes, to compare.'),
   ('What about small forwarders?', 'They can give personal service, but check their capacity and insurance.')],
  ('freight-quote-checker', 'Open the freight quote checker'))
g('cargo-insurance-guide', 'sea', 'Cargo insurance|ICC A, B and C and what is covered',
  'How marine cargo insurance works: ICC (A), (B) and (C) clauses, what is covered, what is excluded and how to claim.',
  'Carriers limit their liability, often to a small amount per kilogram. Cargo insurance covers the real value.',
  [('The three clauses', [('table', ['Clause', 'Cover'], [['ICC (A)', 'All risks, except exclusions. The widest cover.'], ['ICC (B)', 'Named perils such as fire, collision, sea water entry'], ['ICC (C)', 'Narrower named perils, like fire and sinking']])]),
   ('Common exclusions', 'Poor packing, inherent vice, delay, normal leakage and war or strikes unless added. Read the policy.'),
   ('How much to insure', 'The usual rule is CIF value plus 10 percent. Confirm what the buyer or the letter of credit needs.'),
   ('Claims', ['Note the damage on the delivery receipt and tell the carrier in writing at once.', 'Keep the packaging, photos and the survey report.', 'Send the claim papers to the insurer: BL, invoice, packing list, survey and the carrier notice.'])],
  [('Is the carrier liable for damage?', 'Within limits that are low compared with the real value.'),
   ('Who buys insurance under FOB?', 'The buyer.'),
   ('Can I get a single shipment policy?', 'Yes, from the insurer or through your forwarder. Open policies suit regular shippers.')],
  ('cargo-insurance', 'See our cargo insurance'))
g('general-average-explained', 'sea', 'General average|what it is and what to do',
  'What general average is in shipping, how it is declared, what cargo owners must pay and how insurance helps.',
  'General average is an old sea law. When a ship is in danger and part of the cargo or ship is sacrificed to save the rest, all the owners share the loss.',
  [('How it works', 'The master or the owner declares general average. A surveyor, the average adjuster, works out each party share. Cargo owners may have to pay a guarantee before the cargo is released.'),
   ('What you will be asked', 'A general average bond, an average guarantee from your insurer, and the commercial invoice to value the cargo.'),
   ('Insurance', 'A good cargo policy pays your share. Without insurance, you pay from your own pocket and may not get your cargo until you do.'),
   ('Why it matters', 'It can happen even if your cargo is not damaged. It is a main reason to insure every shipment.')],
  [('Is it common?', 'It is rare, but it is expensive when it happens.'),
   ('How long does it take?', 'The adjustment can take many months. The release of cargo is faster when security is given.'),
   ('Who gives the guarantee?', 'Your insurer, if you have cover.')],
  ('cargo-insurance', 'See our cargo insurance'))
g('cbm-calculation-guide', 'sea', 'How to calculate CBM|cubic metres for shipping',
  'How to calculate CBM for cartons and pallets, how it ties to LCL pricing and the volume weight rules for air and courier.',
  'CBM is the cubic metre of your cargo. It is the basis for LCL freight and for planning what fits in a container.',
  [('The formula', 'Length × width × height in metres, times the number of packages. In centimetres: L × W × H ÷ 1,000,000. A carton 60 × 40 × 40 cm is 0.096 CBM.'),
   ('Total', 'Add up the CBM of all packages. Use the outside dimensions of the packed cargo, including pallets.'),
   ('LCL billing', 'LCL is billed per CBM or per tonne, whichever is greater. This is the "weight or measurement" rule. One cubic metre equals one tonne for the comparison.'),
   ('Air freight', 'Air uses a different divisor to convert volume into chargeable weight. See our volumetric weight guide.')],
  [('Do I use inner or outer dimensions?', 'Outer, as packed.'),
   ('What if the cargo is irregular?', 'Use the largest length, width and height, because that is the space it takes.'),
   ('Is there a free tool?', 'Yes. The CBM calculator does it for many items at once.')],
  ('cbm-calculator', 'Open the CBM calculator'))
